\section{Mapping kinds}{

\code{mapping_kind} takes five values, and the distinction between the first
four is what stops a source total being counted twice:
\itemize{
\item \code{"exact"}: one source class, one WHEP target.
\item \code{"aggregate"}: a total the publisher ships alongside its own members.
The aggregate binds; its members are never summed alongside it.
\item \code{"member"}: one of those members. It is recorded so the class is
accounted for rather than silently absent, and is not summed.
\item \code{"sum_member"}: one of several classes that \strong{do} sum to the WHEP
target, the publisher shipping no aggregate for them.
\item \code{"dropped"}: no exact WHEP counterpart. The class is never folded into
a near neighbour; \code{mapping_reason} says what it is and why it could not
be mapped, so a coverage report can count it.
}

Every row that is not \code{"exact"} carries a \code{mapping_reason}, and the
builder aborts on one that does not.
}

\section{The rules these tables encode}{

The tables decide nothing. They are the data form of the vocabulary rules
fixed for the subnational spatialization, and each row's
\code{mapping_reason} names the rule it applies:
\itemize{
\item Crops. Where a publisher ships an aggregate class, that aggregate binds
and its members are never summed alongside it. Maize is grain maize
only, silage and forage maize excluded. A source rice area is paddy and
binds \code{item_prod_code} 27. A class with no exact WHEP counterpart is
dropped and counted, never folded into a near neighbour.
\item Livestock. A published dairy series binds dairy cattle, and non-dairy
is the total minus the dairy series, the unit-year being refused where
that difference is negative. A combined sheep-and-goats class
constrains their sum. Published reference dates are accepted as they
are, which is why the NASS hog inventory keeps its 1 December date. A
combined poultry class constrains the sum of the poultry groups.
}
}

\section{Where the class lists come from}{

Each table enumerates the classes its source actually serves, taken from
the source itself rather than from a reader's defaults:
\itemize{
\item Eurostat: the \code{crops} dimension of \code{apro_cpshr} and \code{apro_cpnhr_h}
(identical 79-class vocabularies), the \code{animals} dimension of
\code{apro_mt_ls_r} (47 classes) and of \code{ef_lsk_poultry} (9 classes), read
off the dissemination API on 2026-09-04.
\item IBGE SIDRA: classification 782 of table 5457 (72 categories, one of
them the all-crops total) and classification 79 of table 3939 (10 herd
types), from the \code{servicodados.ibge.gov.br} metadata endpoint on
2026-09-04, plus table 94, whose single series is milked cows.
\item JRC: the nine crop classes of release 2025.01 of the harmonised EU
subnational crop statistics, as
\code{inst/scripts/prepare_jrc_subnational.R} measured them on the pinned
archive.
\item USDA NASS: the Quick Stats \code{SHORT_DESC} series
\code{\link[=read_admin_stats_nass]{read_admin_stats_nass()}} requests, plus the silage-maize series the
maize rule excludes. This is deliberately \strong{not} an inventory of the
NASS vocabulary: Quick Stats keys tens of thousands of series on
\code{SHORT_DESC}, the bulk dump is the only complete listing of them, and
no offline copy of it ships with this package.
}

\link{crops_eurostat} is the older, label-only Eurostat crop table (13 green
fodder and root-crop codes, no WHEP target). It is left as it is;
\code{admin_items_eurostat} is the vocabulary that carries the mapping, and
covers those 13 codes among its 79.
